        {
          "id": "8f1d3d21-faa5-3700-9300-ca56c85a1d62",
          "content": {
            "id": "8f1d3d21-faa5-3700-9300-ca56c85a1d62",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Offshore wind group Orsted sets 67% discount for $9.4 bln rights issue",
            "description": "<p>STORY: Danish offshore wind developer Orsted has priced its $9.4 billion rights issue at over $10 per share.</p><p>That's over a 66% discount to the stock's closing price on Friday, as it shores up its finances in the face of mounting challenges.</p><p>The company has been grappling with supply chain disruptions, surging interest rates and project delays.\u00a0</p><p>U.S. President Donald Trump's anti-wind policies have also caused problems.</p><p>It's all left its share price down 85% from a 2021 peak.</p><p>Earlier this month, Orsted won shareholder approval for the capital raise.</p><p>It's to help fund U.S. projects that have been thrown into uncertainty.</p><p>At the heart of its financial struggles are the U.S. projects Sunrise Wind and Revolution Wind.\u00a0</p><p>Two-thirds of the new capital is earmarked for Sunrise Wind.</p><p>The project saw potential co-investors flee after the White House ordered Norway's Equinor\u00a0to halt a neighbouring wind farm in April.</p><p>The rights issue closes at the start of next month with trading in the new shares set to start on October 10th.</p>",
            "summary": "STORY: Danish offshore wind developer Orsted has priced its $9.4 billion rights issue at over $10 per share.That's over a 66% discount to the stock's closing price on Friday, as it shores up its finances in the face of mounting challenges.The company has been grappling with supply chain disruptions, surging interest rates and project delays.&nbsp;U.S. President Donald Trump's anti-wind policies have also caused problems.It's all left its share price down 85% from a 2021 peak.Earlier this month, Orsted won shareholder approval for the capital raise.It's to help fund U.S. projects that have been thrown into uncertainty.At the heart of its financial struggles are the U.S. projects Sunrise Wind and Revolution Wind.&nbsp;Two-thirds of the new capital is earmarked for Sunrise Wind.The project saw potential co-investors flee after the White House ordered Norway's Equinor&nbsp;to halt a neighbouring wind farm in April.The rights issue closes at the start of next month with trading in the new shares set to start on October 10th.",
